# AI Speaker Optimization

## Overview
This project explores optimization-driven audio system calibration using Gaussian Process Regression and Bayesian Optimization. The goal was to improve frequency response quality across listening positions by automating what is traditionally a manual tuning process.

## Problem
Speaker placement and toe-in angle can significantly affect room response. Manual tuning is time-consuming and difficult to optimize systematically, especially across multiple listening positions.

## Approach
I modeled speaker-system behavior using Gaussian Processes and used Bayesian Optimization (UCB-based search) to iteratively identify promising configurations. I also developed Harman-inspired scoring functions to quantify room response quality.

## Methods and Tools
- Python
- Gaussian Process Regression
- Bayesian Optimization
- Frequency response analysis
- Objective function design

## Files
- `AI_SpeakerProj.py` — main optimization script
- `Bayesian_Optimization_Speaker_System_Report.docx.pdf` — project report
- `ToeIN_*.txt` — measurements/configuration data
- `spectrum*.csv` — spectral response data

## Key Takeaways
- Demonstrated how probabilistic optimization can automate signal calibration
- Framed speaker tuning as a measurable optimization problem
- Built a reusable workflow for evaluating noisy real-world signal data

## How to Use
1. Review the report for project motivation and methodology
2. Open `AI_SpeakerProj.py` to inspect the optimization workflow
3. Use the `.txt` and `.csv` files as sample experimental inputs
